\section{Read the scale of a prediction}{
The \emph{linear predictor}, or link scale, is the intercept plus each
coefficient times its covariate, plus any formula offset. The \emph{response
scale} applies the model's inverse link function to it. \code{exp()} inverts a
log link and turns a linear predictor into an expected count. \code{plogis()}
inverts a logit link and turns it into a probability. Choose with
\code{type = "link"} or \code{type = "response"}.

To back-transform a link-scale result yourself, apply the inverse link to
the estimate and to each confidence limit. Do not apply it to a standard
error. A response-scale standard error comes from the delta method,
\verb{abs(d mu / d eta) x SE(link)}, where \code{mu} is the expected response and
\code{eta} is the linear predictor. For a log link this is \verb{mu x SE(link)}.
The analytic backends return it with \verb{type = "response", se.fit = TRUE}.
Their confidence limits are computed on the link scale and then
transformed, so on the response scale they are asymmetric and stay inside
the valid range (positive counts, probabilities between 0 and 1).

Some models have their own scales:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Cox and step-selection models.} The linear predictor is a log
relative risk, and \code{exp()} of it is a relative risk or relative selection
score. Neither is a probability of occurrence or survival. Native Cox
refits use \code{type = "lp"} (which \code{type = "link"} also selects) or
\code{type = "risk"}. The conditional logistic backend uses \code{"link"} and
\code{"risk"}, and its \code{"response"} is a probability normalized within each
supplied choice set.
\item \strong{Linear models.} For an \code{lm} refit the link and response scales are
the same, so \code{type = "link"} returns the response.
\item \strong{\code{unmarked} models.} Native prediction back-transforms each component
by default, so abundance is a count and occupancy and detection are
probabilities. Bind \code{native_args = list(backTransform = FALSE)} in
\code{\link[=prepare_prediction]{prepare_prediction()}} to keep the component's link scale.
}

Landscape covariates enter the model standardized by default, so a
coefficient describes the change in the linear predictor for one standard
deviation of the kernel-weighted covariate. Every prediction route reuses
the mean and standard deviation from the fitted data. Never standardize a
prediction data set on its own.
}

\section{Expected response or a new observation}{
The \emph{expected response} is the model's mean at a site: the expected count,
or the probability of occurrence. An observation recorded at that site
scatters around this mean. That scatter is \emph{observation noise}. Every
standard error and interval that \code{\link[=predict.multiScaleR]{predict.multiScaleR()}},
\code{\link[=prepare_prediction]{prepare_prediction()}}, and \code{\link[=plot_marginal_effects]{plot_marginal_effects()}} return describes the
expected response or a model component, and none adds observation noise.
Two native controls are the exceptions:
\itemize{
\item For an \code{lm} refit, \code{native_args = list(interval = "prediction")} returns
limits for a future observation, including the residual variance.
\item For a spaMM refit, the native \code{residVar} and \code{respVar} variance
attributes are on the response scale and include observation noise.
}

multiScaleR has no \code{simulate()} method for \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} fits. For
a GLM, the native \code{simulate(fit$opt_mod)} draws new responses at the fitted
rows from the fitted means, holding the scales and coefficients at their
estimates. For a Bayesian fit, \code{\link[=simulate.multiScaleR_bayes]{simulate.multiScaleR_bayes()}} draws
replicated observations that carry both posterior uncertainty and
observation noise.
}

\section{Choose conditional or joint uncertainty}{
\tabular{lll}{
   Fitting route \tab Joint uncertainty \tab Conditional uncertainty \cr
   \code{backend = "profile_glm"} or \code{"joint_nb2"} \tab Yes, from \code{predict()} at points and from \code{vcov()}, when \code{fit$uncertainty$available} is \code{TRUE}, or when \code{fit$uncertainty$joint_conditional_on} names scales held fixed at a search bound \tab Yes, at points and on rasters \cr
   \code{backend = "conditional_logit"} \tab No \tab Yes \cr
   \code{backend = "joint_mixed"} \tab No \tab Yes, from native glmmTMB \cr
   Default \code{backend = "refit"} \tab No \tab Yes, from the native provider \cr
   \code{\link[=multiScale_bayes]{multiScale_bayes()}} \tab The posterior already covers scales and coefficients together \tab Not applicable \cr
}


\code{predict()} defaults to \code{uncertainty = "joint"}. Every route except the
analytic backends at points needs \code{uncertainty = "conditional"} before it
returns a standard error, and stops otherwise rather than silently
returning a conditional one. For the analytic backends, joint uncertainty
is refused when the fit sits at a parameter bound or fails its
identifiability checks. \code{fit$uncertainty$reasons} says why. Point
predictions remain available in that case. The one exception is a fit
whose only failed check is a kernel parameter at a search bound. Its joint uncertainty holds that parameter fixed at the bound
and lets the other parameters vary, so it sits between conditional and
fully joint uncertainty. Report it as conditional on the bounded scale, and
consider widening the search. At the exact Poisson boundary
of a \code{"joint_nb2"} fit, conditional standard errors and intervals also hold
the dispersion at that boundary.

Effect curves from \code{\link[=plot_marginal_effects]{plot_marginal_effects()}} and predictions from the
native model in \code{fit$opt_mod} are conditional for every backend, including
the analytic ones. A conditional interval answers the narrower question of
how uncertain a prediction is if the scales of effect are exactly their
estimates. Say so when you report one.
}

\section{Confidence intervals and prediction intervals}{
A \emph{confidence interval} describes uncertainty about the expected response.
A \emph{prediction interval} describes where a single new observation may fall.
It adds observation noise and is wider.
\itemize{
\item The analytic backends return Wald confidence limits with
\code{interval = "confidence"}, at the confidence \code{level} you set.
\item Every other route requires \code{interval = "none"} in \code{predict()}. Native
\code{unmarked} tables carry \code{lower} and \code{upper} confidence limits for the
component at the native level, 0.95 unless you bind another \code{level} in
\code{native_args}. An \code{lm} refit returns native mean confidence limits with
\code{native_args = list(interval = "confidence")} and prediction limits with
\code{interval = "prediction"}, both with \code{uncertainty = "conditional"}.
\item Effect-curve bands from \code{\link[=plot_marginal_effects]{plot_marginal_effects()}} are 95\% Wald limits for
the expected response.
\item Bayesian credible intervals from \code{\link[=predict.multiScaleR_bayes]{predict.multiScaleR_bayes()}} describe
the expected response. Intervals from \code{\link[=simulate.multiScaleR_bayes]{simulate.multiScaleR_bayes()}}
replicates describe new observations.
}

Wald intervals are approximations, and the package does not establish
their coverage. \code{vignette("v09-estimates-and-intervals", package = "multiScaleR")} summarizes how well they performed in simulations.
}

\section{Random effects and new groups}{
Native mixed models predict with the fitted random effects of known groups
by default (glmmTMB and lme4). Setting the random effects to zero gives a
reference prediction for a group whose deviation is exactly zero:
\itemize{
\item glmmTMB, lme4, and spaMM: \code{re.form = NA} (or \code{re.form = ~0}).
\item nlme: \code{native_args = list(level = 0)}.
\item \code{unmarked}: any non-\code{NULL} \code{re.form} sets every random effect to zero. It
does not select a subset of effects.
}

A zero-effect prediction is not an average over the random-effect
distribution. Under a nonlinear link, the average over groups generally
differs from the prediction at zero. None of the frequentist routes integrates over
groups or samples effects for new groups. A new group level needs the
provider's own control, such as \code{allow.new.levels = TRUE} for glmmTMB, and
then follows the provider's rules. \code{\link[=plot_marginal_effects]{plot_marginal_effects()}} always sets
random effects to zero. Bayesian predictions can sample new-group effects,
as \code{\link[=predict.multiScaleR_bayes]{predict.multiScaleR_bayes()}} describes.

Pass \code{re.form} and other native controls through \code{...} in
\code{\link[=predict.multiScaleR]{predict.multiScaleR()}}, or bind them with \code{native_args} in
\code{\link[=prepare_prediction]{prepare_prediction()}}. The \code{effects} field of the \link{prediction_contract}
records the policy that was applied.
}

\section{Components of unmarked fits}{
An \code{unmarked} model has several regression components, and a prediction
describes one of them. Name it with \code{type} in \code{\link[=predict.multiScaleR]{predict.multiScaleR()}} and
\code{\link[=plot_marginal_effects]{plot_marginal_effects()}}, or with \code{component} in \code{\link[=prepare_prediction]{prepare_prediction()}}.
Point predictions from \code{occu()} and \code{pcount()} fits default to \code{"state"}.
Snapshots and raster predictions require an explicit component.\tabular{llll}{
   Model \tab \code{type} \tab What it predicts \tab Rows it uses \cr
   \code{unmarked::occu()} \tab \code{"state"} \tab Probability that a site is occupied \tab Sites \cr
   \code{unmarked::occu()} \tab \code{"det"} \tab Probability of detection on one survey, given that the site is occupied \tab Site by survey \cr
   \code{unmarked::pcount()} \tab \code{"state"} \tab Expected abundance at a site. With \code{mixture = "ZIP"}, the overall mean, including structural zeros \tab Sites \cr
   \code{unmarked::pcount()} \tab \code{"det"} \tab Probability of detecting each individual on one survey \tab Site by survey \cr
   \code{unmarked::gdistremoval()} \tab \code{"lambda"} \tab Abundance or density parameter \tab Sites \cr
   \code{unmarked::gdistremoval()} \tab \code{"phi"} \tab Availability, active only with more than one primary period \tab Site by primary period \cr
   \code{unmarked::gdistremoval()} \tab \code{"dist"} \tab Distance-detection parameter of the fitted key, inactive for a uniform key \tab Site by primary period \cr
   \code{unmarked::gdistremoval()} \tab \code{"rem"} \tab Removal parameter \tab Site by primary period by removal interval \cr
}


Abundance from \code{pcount()} counts the animals present, not the animals you
would expect to count. For \code{plot_marginal_effects()}, \code{type = "lambda"} is
an alias for \code{"state"} in \code{occu()} and \code{pcount()} fits, and \code{"state"} is an
alias for \code{"lambda"} in \code{gdistremoval()} fits. Other classes keep their
native component names, such as \code{"psi"}, \code{"col"}, and \code{"ext"} for dynamic
occupancy (initial occupancy, colonization, and extinction). These are
distinct from smoothed or projected occupancy trajectories.

Native standard errors and confidence limits for every component condition
on the selected scales and add no observation noise. A component
prediction does not change the fitted model's parameter count, likelihood,
or diagnostics. \code{vignette("v02-user-guide", package = "multiScaleR")}
fits and maps N-mixture and occupancy models, and
\code{vignette("v12-distance-removal", package = "multiScaleR")} covers
\code{gdistremoval()}.
}

\section{Bayesian fits}{
A fit from \code{\link[=multiScale_bayes]{multiScale_bayes()}} carries posterior draws of the scales and
coefficients together, so its predictions need no conditional or joint
choice. \code{\link[=predict.multiScaleR_bayes]{predict.multiScaleR_bayes()}} returns posterior summaries of the
expected response, \code{\link[=simulate.multiScaleR_bayes]{simulate.multiScaleR_bayes()}} returns replicated
observations, and \code{\link[=predict_scales]{predict_scales()}} returns posterior scales for observed
or new groups. \code{vignette("v14-bayesian-prediction", package = "multiScaleR")} predicts new sites, new years, and totals.
}

\section{Check what a result describes}{
Snapshots from \code{\link[=prepare_prediction]{prepare_prediction()}}, native refit predictions, and
Bayesian summaries carry a \link{prediction_contract} that names the quantity,
its scale, the random-effect policy, and the uncertainty it includes and
omits. Read it when you are unsure which interval you are holding.
}

